  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, i.e., one that concentrates more probability on unfavorable outcomes. As established, Lottery B is more right-skewed and would be preferred by this reason. However, the individual's verbal report shows they considered the probability of avoiding loss (favorable outcome) and found A more attractive due to its 55% chance of no loss versus B’s only 10%. This reasoning opposes the decision reason: the individual is favoring the less right-skewed lottery (A), and their justification is based on minimizing the chance of any loss, not on preferring higher skewness. There is no evidence they assessed the distributional shape or skewness as defined. The information they used (probabilities of no loss) partially overlaps with what is needed for skewness (probabilities of unfavorable outcomes), but their evaluative rule (favor higher chance of no loss) contradicts the reason’s rule (favor more right-skewed lottery). Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

