bability on a loss (albeit moderate), then the reason would prefer B. But this interpretation is questionable. In sum, the reason is poorly applicable, but with a stretch, B might be seen as 'more right-skewed' in a behavioral sense (sure loss), though technically skewness is not defined for a degenerate distribution. The relevant information is: A has a 50% chance of a large loss and 50% chance of no loss; B has a sure moderate loss. The reason would need to assess probability concentration on unfavorable outcomes. A has 50% on unfavorable, B has 100% on unfavorable (since -500 < 0), so B assigns greater probability to unfavorable outcomes. Thus, B is more right-skewed. The reason prefers B.",
