  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one more heavily weighted toward unfavorable outcomes (more right-skewed). As analyzed, B assigns 100% probability to an unfavorable outcome (loss), while A assigns only 50% to an unfavorable outcome, so B is more right-skewed under this definition, and the reason would prefer B. The individual also chooses B, but their justification is not based on the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes as a distributional property. Instead, they focus on minimizing the 'possible loss' and achieving certainty in that minimization. This reflects a risk-averse or certainty-seeking heuristic, such as minimizing maximum loss or preferring sure outcomes, but not an evaluation of skewness. The individual does not mention or imply any consideration of the shape of the distribution, the balance of probabilities across outcomes, or the concept of skewness. They do not note that B puts all probability on a loss while A allows a chance of no loss — even though that is factual, their reasoning centers on the certainty of avoiding a larger loss, not on the increased probability of any loss. Thus, while the outcome aligns with what the reason would recommend (choosing B), the reasoning process is different. The information considered overlaps partially (probability of loss, magnitude), but the integration into a decision rule is not based on skewness. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used the skewness reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
